" (https://shonan.nii.ac.jp/docs/c6021bffb1d13b8e5dac236bebf97a2346591735.pdf)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 23; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://shonan.nii.ac.jp/docs/c6021bffb1d13b8e5dac236bebf97a2346591735.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 916\nL0@P0: ISSN 2186-7437\nL1@P0: NII Shonan Meeting Report\nL2@P0: No. 211\nL3@P0: National Institute of Informatics\nL4@P0: 2-1-2 Hitotsubashi, Chiyoda-Ku, Tokyo, Japan\nL5@P0: Theory and Algorithms in Graph\nL6@P0: Rigidity and Algebraic Statistics\nL7@P0: Fatemeh Mohammadi\nL8@P0: Bernd Schulze\nL9@P0: Meera Sitharam\nL10@P0: Shin-ichi Tanigawa\nL11@P0-1: September 2–6, 2024Theory and Algorithms in Graph\nL12@P1: Rigidity and Algebraic Statistics\nL13@P1: Organizers:\nL14@P1: Fatemeh Mohammadi (KU Leuven)\nL15@P1: Bernd Schulze (Lancaster University)\nL16@P1: Meera Sitharam (University of Florida)\nL17@P1: Shin-ichi Tanigawa (University of Tokyo)\nL18@P1: September 2 – 6, 2024\nL19@P1: Abstract\nL20@P1: The main objective of the proposed meeting is to identify and explore\nL21@P1: key emerging connections between two active algorithmic research areas:\nL22@P1: graph rigidity theory, with applications in diverse geometric modeling sce\u0002narios and algebraic statistics, with applications in data-driven inference\nL23@P1: and machine learning. To this end, the invited experts in each field will\nL24@P1: outline recent fruitful interactions as well as mathematical and computa\u0002tional tools for the fundamental problems in these two areas, to establish\nL25@P1: a common language for participants with different expertise.\nL26@P1: Background and introduction\nL27@P1: Graph rigidity theory and algorithmic characteri\u0002zations\nL28@P1: A fundamental topic in computational geometry is the efficient algorithmic\nL29@P1: characterization of basic properties of point configurations in Euclidean\nL30@P1: space that satisfy various algebraic geometric constraints. Example prop\u0002erties of such feasible sets of point configurations are emptiness, finiteness,\nL31@P1: singularity etc. Rigidity theory establishes an underlying mathematical\nL32@P1: foundation by revealing and studying discrete structures, both geometric\nL33@P1: and purely combinatorial, inherent in geometric constraint systems. This\nL34@P1: is a classical topic in discrete geometry, whose history can be traced back\nL35@P1: to the work of Euler, Cauchy and Maxwell on the rigidity of polyhedra\nL36@P1: and skeletal frames. In the last two decades, both geometric and combina\u0002torial rigidity theory have become particularly active, drawing on diverse\nL37@P1: areas of mathematics and computer science and engaging with a growing\nL38@P1: range of modern applications, such as Computer-Aided-Design, molecular\nL39@P1: modeling, localisation in sensor networks, and the distributed control of\nL40@P1: formations of autonomous agents. As a result, the area has gained sig\u0002nificant international visibility as an active and interdisciplinary research\nL41@P1: area. In Spring 2021 the Fields Institute in Toronto, Canada, ran a 6-\nL42@P1: month thematic program on this topic (Geometric Constraint Systems,\nL43@P1: Framework Rigidity, and Distance Geometry) and an additional 2-month\nL44@P1-2: 1program will take place in Summer 2023. Furthermore, a 6-month pro\u0002gram is planned at ICERM, Brown, USA, in Spring 2025 (Geometry of\nL45@P2: Materials, Packings and Rigid Frameworks).\nL46@P2: Algebraic statistics, emerging connections, timeli\u0002ness\nL47@P2: Recently it has become increasingly clear that rigidity theory and the\nL48@P2: similarly flourishing field of algebraic statistics – which employs algebraic\nL49@P2: approaches to data-driven, statistical and machine learning questions –\nL50@P2: share common goals in capturing hidden discrete structures inherent in\nL51@P2: algebraic relations. Techniques in one field have been shown to shed a\nL52@P2: new light on a challenging problem in the other field. One representa\u0002tive example is the estimation of the probability distribution for a sample\nL53@P2: of observations from a problem domain. Maximum likelihood estima\u0002tion (MLE) is a common framework used throughout the field of machine\nL54@P2: learning. MLE involves maximizing a likelihood function in order to find\nL55@P2: the probability distribution and parameters that best explain the observed\nL56@P2: data, and hence, provides a framework for predictive modeling in machine\nL57@P2: learning where finding model parameters can be formulated as an opti\u0002mization problem. Recently, rigidity theory has been shown to provide\nL58@P2: useful tools in the realm of Bayesian statistics and Gaussian graphical\nL59@P2: models. For example, it is shown that the maximum likelihood threshold\nL60@P2: of a graph, which is the smallest number of data points that guarantees\nL61@P2: that MLE exist almost surely in the Gaussian graphical model, is closely\nL62@P2: connected to rigidity theory.\nL63@P2: In view of this emerging interaction, it is timely to hold an interna\u0002tional workshop in 2024 whose focus and objective is to identify and ex\u0002plore key connections between rigidity theory and algebraic statistics. To\nL64@P2: this end, the invited experts in each field will outline recent mathematical\nL65@P2: and computational tools for the fundamental problems in graph rigidity\nL66@P2: or algebraic statistical inference and modeling, to establish a common\nL67@P2: language for participants with different expertise.\nL68@P2: Problems in the overlap area of algebraic matroids\nL69@P2: A key mathematical concept for facilitating such an interaction would be\nL70@P2: the theory of algebraic matroids. Matroid theory offers a general frame\u0002work for analyzing discrete structures and developing efficient algorithms\nL71@P2: in various fields such as combinatorial optimization and machine learn\u0002ing. Several key questions in rigidity theory and algebraic statistics are\nL72@P2: inter-related through formulations in terms of matroids associated with\nL73@P2: algebraic varieties. Thus the workshop aims to provide an occasion for\nL74@P2: researchers to share the state-of-art techniques for analyzing algebraic\nL75@P2: matroids and discussing future research directions. Specific problems we\nL76@P2: would like to address during the workshop are the following.\nL77@P2: • Combinatorial and efficient algorithmic characterization of indepen\u0002dence/dependence in a variety of algebraic matroids: efficient algo\u0002rithms will be sought for e.g. checking consistency or redundancy\nL78@P2: of geometric constraint systems or algebraic relations that capture\nL79@P2: specific data properties.\nL80@P2: • Geometric and efficient algorithmic characterization of the identifia\u0002bility of algebraic systems in terms of independence/dependence in\nL81@P2-3: 2algebraic matroids: efficient algorithms will be sought for e.g. check\u0002ing completeness of algebraic constraint systems and the identifiabil\u0002ity of data properties from partial measurements from experiments.\nL82@P3: • Development of a common framework for identifying underlying al\u0002gebraic matroids in applications: this contributes to an acceleration\nL83@P3: of applications and a systematization of the theory of algebraic ma\u0002troids.\nL84@P3: Overview of the Meeting\nL85@P3: We hosted 25 participants with diverse expertise who engaged in discus\u0002sions on various aspects of rigidity theory, encompassing both theoretical\nL86@P3: and algorithmic perspectives, as well as its connections to algebraic statis\u0002tics, persistent homology, and tropical geometry. Each participant deliv\u0002ered a lightning talk, while five domain experts—Louis Theran, Atsuhiro\nL87@P3: Nakamoto, Eran Nevo, Tibor Jordan, and Satoshi Murai—gave in-depth\nL88@P3: one-hour overview lectures. Additionally, Eleftherios Kastis, Jan Leg\u0002ersk´y, Alison La Porta, Bill Jackson, D´aniel Garamv¨olgyi, Oliver Clarke,\nL89@P3: and Bernd Schulze presented 30-minute talks.\nL90@P3: Jessica Sidman offered insights into her forthcoming book on rigidity\nL91@P3: theory, tailored for undergraduate and project students, while Jan Leg\u0002ersk´y demonstrated a new computational platform for solving rigidity\u0002related problems, highlighting examples and novel features. Each talk\nL92@P3: was followed by an interactive Q&A session and further discussions, with\nL93@P3: ample time allocated for collaborative working groups over the five days\nL94@P3: of the seminar.\nL95@P3: The social event featured visits to Jomyoji and Hokokuji Temples,\nL96@P3: accompanied by a traditional Japanese tea ceremony.\nL97@P3: Overview of Talks\nL98@P3: Quad-dominated maps on the sphere (and other\nL99@P3: surfaces) and their rigidity properties\nL100@P3: Brigitte Servatius, WPI\nL101@P3: Abstract. Maxwell/Cremona showed that a planar framework can be\nL102@P3: lifted to a polyhedron. Planar rigidity cycles have a stress that is nonzero\nL103@P3: on all edges. Their geometric dual is a rigidity cycle as well and therefore\nL104@P3: the average valence as well as the average face size of such a graph is just\nL105@P3: a bit less than four and in the absence of very large faces the resulting\nL106@P3: polyhedron will be quad-dominated. We suggest a simple procedure to\nL107@P3: easily construct such graphs answering a question of structural engineer\nL108@P3: Bill Baker.\nL109@P3: NAC-colourings\nL110@P3: Anthony Nixon, Lancaster\nL111@P3: Abstract. A NAC-colouring is a type of edge-colouring whose existence\nL112@P3: characterises those graphs that admit a flexible realisation in the plane.\nL113@P3-4: 3In this talk a positive solution was presented to a conjecture on pre\u0002cisely when a minimally rigid graph in the plane has a NAC-colouring.\nL114@P4: This represented joint work with Katie Clinch, Daniel Garamvolgyi, John\nL115@P4: Haslegrave, Tony Huyn and Jan Legersk´y.\nL116@P4: Generation of quadrangulations on surfaces and re\u0002lated topics\nL117@P4: Atsuhiro Nakamoto, Yokohama National University\nL118@P4: Abstract. A quadrangulation on a surface is a simple graph on the surface\nL119@P4: such that each face is bounded by a cycle of length 4. A face contraction\nL120@P4: of a face f of G is to identify two opposite vertices of the boundary 4-cycle\nL121@P4: of f and replace two pairs of multiple edges with two single edges respec\u0002tively. A generating theorem of quadrangulations on the sphere states that\nL122@P4: every quadrangulation on the sphere can be reduced to a cycle of length 4\nL123@P4: by a repeated application of face contractions, through quadrangulations.\nL124@P4: In my talk, we introduce various generating theorems of quadrangulations\nL125@P4: on surfaces, and ones with some combinatorial conditions, together with\nL126@P4: generating theorems of triangulations and Eulerian triangulations.\nL127@P4: Finally, focusing on the relation that every quadrangulation on the\nL128@P4: sphere is a bipartite (2, 4)-tight graph, we introduce a generating theorem\nL129@P4: of bipartite (2, 2)-tight graphs, which is related to a bipartite quadrangu\u0002lation on the projective plane.\nL130@P4: Rigidity over the rational numbers\nL131@P4: Sean Dewar, University of Bristol\nL132@P4: Abstract. Let us say that a rational framework (G, p) – a pair made up of a\nL133@P4: simple graph G = (V, E) and a realisation p : V → Q\nL134@P4: d\nL135@P4: – is rationally rigid\nL136@P4: if there are finitely many frameworks (G, q) (modulo isometries) whereby\nL137@P4: ∥pv − pw∥ = ∥qv − qw∥. With this, we say that a graph G is rigid (resp.,\nL138@P4: flexible) in Q\nL139@P4: d\nL140@P4: if there exists an open dense subset of rationally rigid (resp.,\nL141@P4: not rationally rigid) realisations of G in (Q\nL142@P4: d\nL143@P4: )\nL144@P4: V\nL145@P4: . If a graph is rigid in R\nL146@P4: d\nL147@P4: ,\nL148@P4: it is easy to show that it is also rigid in Q\nL149@P4: d\nL150@P4: . The reverse, however, gets a\nL151@P4: little weird. If we take a graph G with 1 degree of freedom in R\nL152@P4: d\nL153@P4: (i.e., G is\nL154@P4: not rigid in R\nL155@P4: d but G +e is for some non-edge e), then its rigidity in Qd\nL156@P4: is\nL157@P4: dependent on the genus of a generic realisation’s configuration space (the\nL158@P4: algebraic curve of edge-length equivalent realisations in (C\nL159@P4: d\nL160@P4: )\nL161@P4: V modulo\nL162@P4: isometries), which now say is the d-genus of the graph. Specifically, if G\nL163@P4: has 1 d.o.f. in R\nL164@P4: d\nL165@P4: , then G is rigid in Q\nL166@P4: d\nL167@P4: if its d-genus is 2 or more as a\nL168@P4: consequence of Falting’s theorem [10, 11], a deep result that is central in\nL169@P4: Algebraic Number Theory. Furthermore, G is flexible in Q\nL170@P4: d\nL171@P4: if its d-genus\nL172@P4: is 0. If the d-genus is 1, it is entirely unclear what happens.\nL173@P4: The first-order flexibility of a periodic framework\nL174@P4: Eleftherios Kastis, Lancaster University\nL175@P4: Abstract. Given a periodic placement p of a Z\nL176@P4: d\nL177@P4: -symmetric graph G, the\nL178@P4: rigidity matrix of the resulting framework G is unitarily equivalent to a\nL179@P4: multiplication operator ψG. This operator generalizes the notion of the\nL180@P4: orbit matrix, providing a route to factor periodic flexes. Moreover, it gives\nL181@P4-5: 4rise to the C(z)-module M(G) generated by its rows, so the infinitesimal\nL182@P5: flex space F(G, C) of G can be identified with the dual annihilator of M(G).\nL183@P5: Using spectral synthesis tools (Lefrank, 1958), it was shown that F(G, C)\nL184@P5: contains a dense subset of pg-sequences uω,h : Z\nL185@P5: d → Cd V0 of the form\nL186@P5: uω,h : k → ω\nL187@P5: k\nL188@P5: h(k)\nL189@P5: where ω ∈ C\nL190@P5: d\nL191@P5: ∗ lies in the so-called geometric flex spectrum of G and h(z)\nL192@P5: is a vector-valued polynomial. A natural question is to extend the above\nL193@P5: results to Γ-symmetric frameworks, where Γ is a finitely generated abelian\nL194@P5: group. This is joint work with S. C. Power.\nL195@P5: Almost periodic rigidity for symmetric frameworks\nL196@P5: Eleftherios Kastis, Lancaster University\nL197@P5: Abstract. Let Γ be an abelian group. Given a Γ-symmetric graph G,\nL198@P5: we can define a pair (G0, m), where G0 = (V0, E0) be a finite directed\nL199@P5: multigraph and m : E0 → Γ is the so-called gain map that encodes the\n"
